#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "15d21b01-bbad-507d-8b3c-1052ebba30eb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "6dea6cd8-9392-5c72-bd4e-af7404c07d17" :question/math-academy-id "e-3457" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $2\\sin x+1=0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sin{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sin x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sin x &= -1 \\\\\n\\sin x &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (-\\frac{1}{2}) =-\\frac{π}{6}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\nx =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nis not in the range\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nTo get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we simply add $2 \\pi$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= -\\frac{π}{6} + 2π = \\frac{11π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/913/Source/Images/b170f4c1e6d9e1c8a09d09c59b5ba53c.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "532584f8-f656-5e25-a86c-6e272468dd6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3458" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\sin x - \\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-180^{\\circ} < x \\leq 180^{\\circ}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sin x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sin x - \\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sin x &= \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\sin x &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) = 60^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $60^{\\circ}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 60^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 60^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 60^{\\circ}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/913/Source/Images/2db14a4844cbf6c495543c6905847053.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 180^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 60^{∘} \\\\\n&= 120^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = 60^\\circ, 120^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "da4b0fce-026c-50b0-ade0-3cb753027d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3460" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\sin{x} = 1$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (1) = 90^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $90^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ + n (360^\\circ)\n$$\n\nSince the domain\n\n$$\n-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nis one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that\n\n$$\nx = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nis the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091932 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "eaee28f0-8458-59b2-a4d1-e86e3dcda3d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3511" :question/problem "Calculate, in radians to $1$ decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\sin x = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{4}{5}) = 0.927295… \\approx 0.927\n$$\n\nSince $0.927$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.927\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.927\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.927\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/913/Source/Images/14ea617c25681a19eca6f44a19ff4aa8.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.927 \\\\\n&= 2.215\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are\n\n$$\nx = 0.9, 2.2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b00527b3-9595-5ea6-84bf-0ac63d49a44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091326} {:answer/id #uuid "d890a5c0-1b6b-51c7-bf4c-2723786159ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091327} {:answer/id #uuid "ee125ca2-c57f-5890-b66a-ff3aac9755b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091328} {:answer/id #uuid "22909b5a-6149-510a-9177-049e3a5fbb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091329} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2d624c-ff8f-5903-bfe2-80af73733105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{3π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d890a5c0-1b6b-51c7-bf4c-2723786159ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091327} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e8fc8c-6d96-54ad-91e7-77f0561da5a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091325}] :question/id #uuid "3724924f-e528-5763-adca-bd553bee798e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8576" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $2\\sin x + 2 = 0$ for $- π < x \\le π$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911c3709-947b-5463-b26f-c374df42a188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091332} {:answer/id #uuid "9bafd6b7-3616-59a8-854c-cc5d09e9c241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091333} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bdab81-d509-53e0-a688-4e5ef7e